WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.294 --> 00:00:08.031
One morning Mayor Madeleine was at work when his secretary announced that Javert wanted to see him. Madeleine frowned.

2
00:00:08.032 --> 00:00:13.134
He had not seen the disagreeable policeman since their confrontation over Fantine.

3
00:00:13.135 --> 00:00:16.126
"Tell him to come in," said the mayor.

4
00:00:16.127 --> 00:00:22.285
Javert entered the room. The mayor remained seated at his desk, making notes on some papers.

5
00:00:22.286 --> 00:00:27.610
At last Madeleine looked up and said, "Well, Javert, what do you want?"

6
00:00:27.611 --> 00:00:33.031
Javert stayed silent for a moment longer with a sad expression on his face.

7
00:00:33.032 --> 00:00:36.798
Then he said, "A criminal act has been committed, Monsieur Mayor.

8
00:00:36.799 --> 00:00:41.182
A lowly government agent has committed a crime against a magistrate."

9
00:00:41.183 --> 00:00:44.027
"Really? Who is this government agent?"

10
00:00:44.028 --> 00:00:46.894
"I am." Javert lowered his eyes.

11
00:00:46.895 --> 00:00:50.174
"You? And who is this magistrate?"

12
00:00:50.175 --> 00:00:56.587
"You." Javert continued to look at the floor. "I've come to ask you to fire me.

13
00:00:56.588 --> 00:01:00.825
You may argue that I should simply resign, but that's not punishment enough for what I've done."

14
00:01:00.826 --> 00:01:06.833
Madeleine looked puzzled. "What's all this nonsense? What crime have you committed against me?"

15
00:01:06.834 --> 00:01:13.658
"Several weeks ago, after that episode in the police station with Fantine, I reported you to the chief of police in Paris."

16
00:01:13.659 --> 00:01:23.896
The mayor, who did not laugh often, began to laugh now. "Why? Because you felt I overstepped my authority as mayor?"

17
00:01:23.897 --> 00:01:27.694
"No, I reported you for being a former convict."

18
00:01:27.695 --> 00:01:30.721
Madeleine sat up straight in his chair.

19
00:01:30.722 --> 00:01:34.475
Javert, who still had not raised his eyes, continued.

20
00:01:34.476 --> 00:01:40.618
"For a long time I'd been suspicious because you reminded me of a convict from my days as a prison ship guard.

21
00:01:40.619 --> 00:01:48.690
You look like him, and you're incredibly strong just as he was. I mistook you for a man named Jean Valjean."

22
00:01:48.691 --> 00:01:51.468
The mayor started. "Who?"

23
00:01:51.469 --> 00:01:53.789
"A convict named Jean Valjean.

24
00:01:53.790 --> 00:02:00.285
Apparently after he was released, he robbed a bishop's home and then used a weapon to rob a chimney sweep along a deserted road.

25
00:02:00.286 --> 00:02:05.111
For years his whereabouts have been unknown, although the police have been looking for him.

26
00:02:05.112 --> 00:02:09.952
I was angry at you after you let Fantine go, so I wrote to the Paris police."

27
00:02:09.953 --> 00:02:13.857
Madeleine had gathered up his papers again and was busy reading them.

28
00:02:13.858 --> 00:02:19.913
Javert's facts were not quite right, but the policeman's words were still cause for alarm.

29
00:02:19.914 --> 00:02:23.596
"And what answer did you get from the police?" Madeleine asked.

30
00:02:23.597 --> 00:02:28.850
"That I was crazy. And I guess I must be because the real Jean Valjean has been found."

31
00:02:28.851 --> 00:02:33.755
The papers fell from the mayor's hand, and he looked steadily at Javert.

32
00:02:33.756 --> 00:02:37.139
"Ah," he said with no emotion in his voice.

33
00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:40.501
"Let me tell you what happened," said Javert.

34
00:02:40.502 --> 00:02:46.247
He explained that out in the country there lived a simple old man named Champmathieu.

35
00:02:46.248 --> 00:02:51.739
He was very poor, and no one paid much attention to him or how he managed to live.

36
00:02:51.740 --> 00:02:58.755
But he was recently arrested for stealing apples. He even had a branch of the apple tree in his hand when he was caught.

37
00:02:58.756 --> 00:03:05.731
The story should have ended there. But the jail was in need of repair, so he was taken to a prison in Arras.

38
00:03:05.732 --> 00:03:10.396
There, a convict named Brevet recognized him as Valjean.

39
00:03:10.397 --> 00:03:18.057
Since Valjean was such a notorious criminal, the police wanted to be sure. So they brought two more convicts to Arras.

40
00:03:18.058 --> 00:03:26.954
Their names were Cochepaille and Chenildieu, and they were both serving life sentences. Both of them recognized Valjean too.

41
00:03:26.955 --> 00:03:29.127
"Really," said the mayor.

42
00:03:29.128 --> 00:03:32.859
"Yes, and I also recognized him when I went to Arras.

43
00:03:32.860 --> 00:03:39.779
Now that I've seen him, I don't know how I could have mistaken you for him. I beg your pardon, Monsieur Mayor."

44
00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:44.541
"But what did this man say when he was accused of being Valjean?"

45
00:03:44.542 --> 00:03:49.894
"Anyone else would have protested violently, but he pretended he didn't understand what was going on.

46
00:03:49.895 --> 00:03:55.358
Oh, he's a clever rascal. It's a good thing he'll be going away for life."

47
00:03:55.359 --> 00:03:57.664
"For life?" said the mayor.

48
00:03:57.665 --> 00:04:06.525
"Oh, yes. Stealing apples is usually only a minor crime, but for a convict, it's a major crime punishable by a life sentence on the prison ships.

49
00:04:06.526 --> 00:04:09.017
I'm going to Arras to give my testimony."

50
00:04:09.018 --> 00:04:15.517
"All these details are of little interest to me," said Madeleine. "We both have many other things to do.

51
00:04:15.518 --> 00:04:21.104
I have at least four or five complaints here that I'd like you to investigate. When did you say you're giving testimony?"

52
00:04:21.105 --> 00:04:27.914
"I'm leaving tonight because the trial is tomorrow. The judge will decide the case by tomorrow night, but I won't wait around for that.

53
00:04:27.915 --> 00:04:30.834
I know Valjean will be convicted."

54
00:04:30.835 --> 00:04:35.334
"Then go." Mayor Madeleine dismissed Javert with a wave of his hand.

55
00:04:35.335 --> 00:04:40.326
"But you haven't fired me," Javert protested. "I deserve to lose my job.

56
00:04:40.327 --> 00:04:45.905
I've often been harsh on others when they have done wrong. So it's only right that I should be treated harshly too."

57
00:04:45.906 --> 00:04:49.826
"We'll see about that." The mayor extended his hand to Javert.

58
00:04:49.827 --> 00:04:56.890
"A mayor shouldn't shake hands with a spy because that's all I am." Javert bowed and then he left the mayor's office.

59
00:04:56.891 --> 00:05:02.906
At first Madeleine thought that he should rush to Arras and save the prisoner named Champmathieu.

60
00:05:02.907 --> 00:05:10.382
He made arrangements for a carriage to pick him up early the next morning. But when he arrived home, he had second thoughts.

61
00:05:10.383 --> 00:05:16.628
He had always feared that someday he would hear that dreaded name, Jean Valjean, again.

62
00:05:16.629 --> 00:05:23.953
And now it had happened. Madeleine stayed up for most of the night, agonizing over what he should do.

63
00:05:23.954 --> 00:05:32.382
"Javert, who has been suspicious of me for so long, figured things out," the mayor said as he paced by the fire.

64
00:05:32.383 --> 00:05:35.945
"But this terrible bloodhound is off the scent now.

65
00:05:35.946 --> 00:05:43.350
He's satisfied that he knows who the real Valjean is, so he won't bother me anymore. The problem is solved."

66
00:05:43.351 --> 00:05:51.016
But Madeleine did not feel happy, so he thought some more. Perhaps if he admitted that he was Valjean, he would be pardoned.

67
00:05:51.017 --> 00:05:58.842
After all, he had lived a virtuous life ever since he met the bishop. Suddenly he felt as if the bishop were there with him, guiding him.

68
00:05:58.843 --> 00:06:04.421
"I'll do the right thing and save poor Champmathieu," vowed Madeleine.

69
00:06:04.422 --> 00:06:11.588
Then he remembered the coin he'd stolen from Little Gervais. That offense would condemn him to hard labor for life.

70
00:06:11.589 --> 00:06:16.398
And then he thought of Fantine. What would become of her if he went to prison?

71
00:06:16.399 --> 00:06:21.334
What kind of life would her unfortunate daughter have with the Thenardiers?

72
00:06:21.335 --> 00:06:28.159
Other people would be affected too. "My factory will go to ruin, and my employees will be out of work.

73
00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:31.644
The whole region will suffer if I am sent to prison again."

74
00:06:31.645 --> 00:06:39.818
For five hours the mayor paced in front of the fire. Finally exhausted, he dropped into his chair and fell asleep.

75
00:06:39.819 --> 00:06:43.977
"Monsieur Mayor!" called his housekeeper as she rapped on the door.

76
00:06:43.978 --> 00:06:46.947
He struggled awake. "What do you want?"

77
00:06:46.948 --> 00:06:49.636
"It's five o'clock and your carriage is here."

78
00:06:49.637 --> 00:06:51.106
"What carriage?"

79
00:06:51.107 --> 00:06:54.810
"The driver said you ordered a carriage to take you to Arras."

80
00:06:54.811 --> 00:07:01.961
There was a long silence while the mayor stood up and ran his fingers through his hair. He knew what he had to do.

81
00:07:01.962 --> 00:07:05.303
"What shall I say to the driver?" asked the housekeeper.

82
00:07:05.304 --> 00:07:07.922
"Tell him I'll be ready in a few minutes."

